# Local research/cannibalization scratch (never commit cloned fleet repos)
.research/

# Editors / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# Go
bin/

# Node
node_modules/

# Terraform state (never commit)
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform/
*.tfvars

# Dependency lock files are generated per-environment against the local
# provider cache; this repo's cache holds re-packed providers whose checksums
# differ from registry releases, so locks are not committed (regenerate at
# deploy time with `terraform init`).
.terraform.lock.hcl

# Verify-gate attestation artifacts (generated by make verify). The schema the
# attestation must conform to (issue #882) is a checked-in contract; it lives
# under governance/isolation/ (not .verify/), so the whole generated root can
# stay plainly ignored with no negation carve-out.
.verify/
governance/board/violations.jsonl

# Runtime surface state: the rollback overlay the console's surface reader
# consults (issue #802) is host state — an engaged rollback belongs to the
# running deployment, never to a commit.
.rollout/

# Claim locks are runtime state (the claim ledger itself is tracked evidence)
.board/locks/

# The per-event claim DIRECTORY is runtime state too (issue #848). Rule 14 makes
# `claim` mandatory, and `governance/dispatch/claims.py` writes one event file per
# claim into `.board/claims/` — so a lane that merely obeyed the doctrine carried
# an untracked, unignored path that `check-gitignore` refused by name, and could
# not reach a green gate of record no matter what it changed. The frozen legacy
# ledger `.board/claims.jsonl` stays TRACKED: it is the accumulated evidence the
# per-event files are appended to, and ignoring it would take that evidence out of
# review.
/.board/claims/

# The out-of-epic pool rail is runtime state too (epic #707, lane F6/#721): it
# records which issues the active focus parked and is rewritten on every
# drain, so committing it would put a moving dispatch signal in the diff.
# The decision log is the branch/PR history; the rail is the working state.
.board/pool.jsonl

# The dispatch audit trail (issue #885, #1062) is runtime state too: one
# arbitration-outcome record per claim/release, written by claims.arbitrate().
.board/dispatch-audit.jsonl

# Fleet steering mailboxes are runtime state (schema + standing directive are tracked)
.fleet/inbox/
.fleet/outbox/
.fleet/sent/
.fleet/done/
.fleet/slog.jsonl
# Brain-rung surfaces (operator orders, replies) and rung liveness: runtime state.
.fleet/brain/
.fleet/*.heartbeat.json
.fleet/*.lock
# Run registry (which loop is tracking which directive) + report dedupe: runtime state.
.fleet/runs/
.fleet/reported/
# Lane isolation records: runtime state, one file per minted session (issue #263)
.fleet/lanes/
# Lifecycle journals: durable per-item evidence of what was verified and merged
.fleet/lifecycle/
# Session heartbeats: one per running lane, swept by governance/reconcile (#304)
.fleet/sessions/
# Watchdog remedy ledger (tracked module: fleet/watchdog.py, issue #773): one
# record per rung holding its attempt count and escalation, plus the
# one-per-incident escalation artifacts. Runtime state, never committed.
.fleet/watchdog/
# Fleet monitor runtime output (tracked module: fleet/monitor.py)
.fleet/open-eye.log
.fleet/open-eye.heartbeat
# Per-rung capture logs: each rung's stdout+stderr, appended by the spawner
# (tracked modules: fleet/watchdog.py, fleet/control.py, fleet/console.py)
.fleet/brain.log
.fleet/sister.log
.fleet/monitor.log
# Fleet retention (tracked module: fleet/prune.py, issue #280): the telemetry
# ledger, every rotated generation of an append-only ledger, and the pruner's
# own cron log are all runtime state, never committed.
.fleet/runs.jsonl
# Per-run telemetry ledger (tracked module: fleet/runslog.py, issue #219):
# runtime state, one JSONL record per run, never committed.
.fleet/telemetry.jsonl
.fleet/*.jsonl.*
.fleet/prune.log
.fleet/watchdog.log
# Fleet cron rungs' own logs (tracked module: fleet/cron.py, issue #241/#830):
# `ao-fleet-reconcile`'s and `ao-fleet-reap`'s cron logs are runtime state,
# never committed, like the prune/watchdog logs above.
.fleet/reconcile.log
.fleet/reap.log
# Singleton leases (tracked modules: fleet/prune.py issue #280 pattern,
# fleet/watchdog.py issue #773, governance/reconcile/cli.py issue #304): each
# job's own lease file records its last-run heartbeat so a concurrent tick
# can tell a stale lease from a live one — runtime state, never committed.
.fleet/watchdog.lease
.fleet/reconcile.lease
# Reconciliation ledger (tracked module: governance/reconcile/ledger.py,
# issue #304): one append-only JSONL record per reconcile pass, runtime
# state under the reconciled repository root, never committed.
.fleet/reconcile/ledger.jsonl

# Local environment / secrets
.env
.env.*
